1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:01,760
Your house has no chimney.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:06,430
Your bed is made of straw, and you're sharing it with your entire family.

3
00:00:06,430 --> 00:00:09,910
And just on the other side of the room, there's a cow.

4
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:11,760
That's not a punishment.

5
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:13,980
That's your heating system.

6
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:16,610
Welcome to winter, in medieval England.

7
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:18,500
You have five months to survive.

8
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:20,810
And see that meter in the corner?

9
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:23,050
Those are your odds of making it to spring.

10
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:26,050
They're about to have a very bad November.

11
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:33,440
And stick around, because in one of these winters, it got so cold that people were buying their wine by the pound.

12
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:35,250
Let's start with November.

13
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:38,990
The old English name for this month was Blotmonath.

14
00:00:38,990 --> 00:00:40,210
Blood month.

15
00:00:40,210 --> 00:00:41,580
And the name is honest.

16
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:45,540
Around now, families slaughtered a lot of their animals.

17
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:47,070
Not out of cruelty.

18
00:00:47,070 --> 00:00:47,740
Out of math.

19
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:49,880
Grass stops growing in winter.

20
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:50,930
Hay is limited.

21
00:00:50,930 --> 00:00:55,320
You can't feed every animal until spring, so you keep the ones you need,

22
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:59,660
the breeding stock and the ones that pull the plough, and the rest become dinner.

23
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:04,180
And because there's no fridge, the meat gets salted, smoked or dried.

24
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:08,590
A family pig, killed around Saint Martin's Day, on the 11th of November,

25
00:01:08,590 --> 00:01:13,120
could be the difference between eating meat this winter and not.

26
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:15,090
Very, very salty meat.

27
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:16,460
For months.

28
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:18,340
Now, let's look at your house.

29
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:19,420
One main room.

30
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:24,300
Walls of wattle and daub, which is woven sticks covered in mud and dung.

31
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:25,500
A thatched roof.

32
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:28,230
And in the middle of the floor, an open fire.

33
00:01:28,230 --> 00:01:29,140
No chimney.

34
00:01:29,140 --> 00:01:38,410
Chimneys and fireplaces were spreading in richer homes, but in a lot of ordinary houses the smoke just drifted up into the roof and found its own way out.

35
00:01:38,410 --> 00:01:39,840
Eventually.

36
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:43,080
So you're always either too smoky or too cold.

37
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:44,300
Usually both.

38
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:47,310
Your face is roasting and your back is freezing.

39
00:01:47,310 --> 00:01:51,700
And in some parts of England, like Dartmoor, people lived in longhouses.

40
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:56,210
One long building, with the family at one end, a passage in the middle,

41
00:01:56,210 --> 00:02:01,320
and the cattle at the other end, under the same roof, especially in the coldest months.

42
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:02,040
Is it smelly?

43
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,040
Absolutely.

44
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:08,210
But a cow is basically a big, warm radiator that eats grass.

45
00:02:08,210 --> 00:02:11,190
In January, you stop complaining about the smell.

46
00:02:11,190 --> 00:02:15,700
As for clothes, the strategy is simple: wear everything you own.

47
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:20,140
A linen shirt, wool tunics on top of each other, wool hose on your legs,

48
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:22,610
a cloak, and a hood if you have one.

49
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:28,800
Women spun the wool themselves, so every layer you're wearing took someone in your family hours to make.

50
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:30,720
And your shoes are thin leather.

51
00:02:30,810 --> 00:02:32,400
There are no rubber boots.

52
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,280
Every time you go outside, your feet get wet.

53
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,520
And there is no dryer.

54
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:41,490
Just the smoky fire, and a lot of patience.

55
00:02:41,490 --> 00:02:43,060
Here's something we forget.

56
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:48,170
In the middle of an English winter, the sun sets before four in the afternoon.

57
00:02:48,170 --> 00:02:49,800
And then it's dark.

58
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:50,880
Really dark.

59
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:52,910
No electricity, obviously.

60
00:02:52,910 --> 00:02:58,000
Good candles were made of beeswax, and those were for churches and rich people.

61
00:02:58,090 --> 00:03:03,760
Beeswax could cost about three times as much as tallow, which is candles made from animal fat.

62
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:07,170
And a lot of poor families couldn't even afford that.

63
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:13,210
They used rushlights: dried rushes dipped in fat, burning for a few minutes each.

64
00:03:13,210 --> 00:03:16,720
Your whole evening, lit by a greasy plant.

65
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:20,310
And then a bell rings, and it's time to cover the fire,

66
00:03:20,310 --> 00:03:24,160
to keep it from burning down the house, and the whole village with it.

67
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:27,630
That bell had a name in Old French: cuevrefeu.

68
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:29,030
"Cover fire".

69
00:03:29,030 --> 00:03:31,600
Which is where we get the word "curfew".

70
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:33,110
So, bedtime.

71
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:37,680
Your mattress is a sack of straw, on the floor or up in a loft.

72
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:39,440
And you're not alone in it.

73
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:43,680
Families slept together, huddled up, because body heat is free.

74
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:45,520
And here's where it gets interesting.

75
00:03:45,610 --> 00:03:52,320
According to historian Roger Ekirch, for centuries, Europeans often didn't sleep in one long block.

76
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:56,960
They had a "first sleep", then woke up in the middle of the night for an hour or so,

77
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:59,440
and then went back for a "second sleep".

78
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:05,100
In that gap, people prayed, did small chores, talked, visited neighbors, and,

79
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:07,280
well, made more peasants.

80
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:11,520
With fourteen hours of darkness, you'd probably wake up in the middle too.

81
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:16,000
Then comes December, and the best news of the whole winter: Christmas.

82
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,120
And for you, Christmas is a big deal, because it's one of the few times in the year when the work actually stops.

83
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:27,280
On a lot of manors, tenants owed the lord some rent at Christmas,

84
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:28,990
often paid in food.

85
00:04:28,990 --> 00:04:30,880
Hens, bread, ale.

86
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:34,400
So you walk up to the big house, holding your chickens.

87
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,340
And on some manors, there was a feast for the tenants.

88
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:41,840
But the tradition, according to some accounts, was that you brought your own plate.

89
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:43,520
And your own firewood.

90
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:47,920
Still, it's meat, it's warm, and nobody is making you work.

91
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:51,270
Twelve days of Christmas, right through to Twelfth Night.

92
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:54,800
And then, on the first Monday after, the party's over.

93
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:57,680
In parts of England this was called Plough Monday.

94
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:01,280
Which tells you exactly what you're doing that day.

95
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:03,100
Now we get to the hard part.

96
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:05,660
Some medieval winters were brutal.

97
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:14,520
In 1410, according to a London chronicle, a frost lasted fourteen weeks and the Thames froze so solid that people could walk,

98
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:16,690
and even ride horses, across it.

99
00:05:16,690 --> 00:05:21,840
In the winter of 1281 to 1282, when the ice finally broke,

100
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:24,140
it smashed arches of London Bridge.

101
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:26,710
People had been crossing the river on foot.

102
00:05:26,710 --> 00:05:29,110
Dry-shod.

103
00:05:29,110 --> 00:05:32,680
And the winter of 1205 is the one I promised you.

104
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:37,360
A frost that started in mid-January and lasted until late March.

105
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:39,180
No one could work the ground.

106
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:48,110
And, according to an old account of great English frosts, ale and wine froze so hard that they were sold by weight.

107
00:05:48,110 --> 00:05:51,600
You haven't truly lived until you've had to chew your beer.

108
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:58,000
In the winter of 1434, the Thames froze from London Bridge all the way down to Gravesend,

109
00:05:58,090 --> 00:06:00,600
from Christmas Day to the 10th of February.

110
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:05,410
Ships couldn't get through, so goods had to be hauled into London overland.

111
00:06:05,410 --> 00:06:10,400
Which, in medieval roads covered in snow, is about as fun as it sounds.

112
00:06:10,400 --> 00:06:13,200
And if you want to see what February looked like back then,

113
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:14,720
there's a famous painting.

114
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:19,520
In a French prayer book made for a duke around 1412 to 1416,

115
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:23,890
Les Très Riches Heures, the page for February shows a snowy farm.

116
00:06:23,890 --> 00:06:25,740
Sheep huddled in their pen.

117
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:27,400
A man hauling firewood.

118
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:31,830
And peasants inside, sitting as close to the fire as humanly possible,

119
00:06:31,830 --> 00:06:33,120
trying to thaw out.

120
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:38,800
Six hundred years later, it's still the most relatable painting of winter ever made.

121
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,420
And here's the real danger.

122
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:44,200
When the ground freezes for months, you can't plough or sow.

123
00:06:44,200 --> 00:06:47,240
And a late spring means a late, small harvest.

124
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:51,600
Which means next winter, you start with even less.

125
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:54,320
If you make it to March, congratulations.

126
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:56,700
You've reached the most dangerous part.

127
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:59,470
Historians sometimes call it the hungry gap.

128
00:06:59,470 --> 00:07:01,400
Your salted meat is running out.

129
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:03,120
The grain is running low.

130
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,880
And the new crops won't be ready for months.

131
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,340
The first wild greens are only just starting to show up.

132
00:07:09,340 --> 00:07:15,220
And the Church timing doesn't help: this is also Lent, forty days of fasting with no meat.

133
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:18,740
So your diet becomes salted herring.

134
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:20,010
And hope.

135
00:07:20,010 --> 00:07:22,740
And sometimes, it's not one bad winter.

136
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:24,800
It's a string of bad years.

137
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:31,200
Starting in 1315, it rained, and rained, and stayed cold, across Northern Europe.

138
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:34,220
Crops rotted in the fields, year after year.

139
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:36,910
It became known as the Great Famine.

140
00:07:36,910 --> 00:07:41,130
It hit a region of around thirty million people, from Ireland to Poland,

141
00:07:41,130 --> 00:07:45,200
and the crops kept failing until the harvest of 1317.

142
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:48,970
And right after it came a cattle plague that, in some places,

143
00:07:48,970 --> 00:07:50,720
killed most of the herds.

144
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:53,360
The cows that kept you warm, and alive.

145
00:07:54,090 --> 00:07:58,240
Some historians see this as the start of a colder era in Europe,

146
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:00,640
often called the Little Ice Age.

147
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:08,000
Exactly when it began is still debated, but your grandchildren's winters weren't getting any easier.

148
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,690
But let's say you made it.

149
00:08:09,690 --> 00:08:10,760
The snow melts.

150
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:12,220
The days get longer.

151
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:14,320
The first green shoots come up.

152
00:08:14,410 --> 00:08:17,120
The cow, somehow, is still alive.

153
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:25,440
You've survived five months of smoke, salt, darkness, frozen beer and a bed shared with six people and a cow.

154
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:30,080
And in about seven months, you get to do it all again.

155
00:08:30,170 --> 00:08:33,679
So, be honest: how many months would you have lasted?

156
00:08:33,679 --> 00:08:34,539
November?

157
00:08:34,539 --> 00:08:35,470
February?

158
00:08:35,470 --> 00:08:36,720
Tell me in the comments.

159
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:39,760
And if you want to find out what else you wouldn't survive,

160
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:40,559
you know what to do.

161
00:08:41,679 --> 00:08:43,919
Red needs all the help he can get.
